WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:06.895
[Narrator] The cult Netflix
series "Stranger Things"

00:00:06.895 --> 00:00:09.220
is back and darker than ever.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.140
The last series was set in 1984,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.960
but this one jumps forward to 1985,

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.160
following a group of now young adults.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.950
Seasons one and two were filled
with great '80s references.

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:24.943
We're gonna have a look at
all the ones in season three.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.870
And yes, spoilers are coming.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.600
Man: A beauty, a jock, a rebel.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.583
[Narrator] 1985 was a
strong year for cult cinema.

00:00:36.583 --> 00:00:38.830
Box-office hits included "The Goonies,"

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.690
"Brazil," and "Teen Wolf," so we expected

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:44.950
some strong cultural
references from season three.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.358
The most obvious one is

00:00:46.358 --> 00:00:48.620
Robert Zemeckis' "Back to the Future."

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.420
This features obviously in episode seven,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.161
when Dustin and his group hide
in the screening of the movie

00:00:54.161 --> 00:00:55.902
to escape the Soviets.

00:00:55.902 --> 00:00:57.922
First, we see a poster,

00:00:57.922 --> 00:01:00.960
then segments of the movie
playing on the projector.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.170
But did you spot an earlier reference

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:05.940
in Mrs. Driscoll's house in episode two?

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.920
The camera pans from a
Kit-Cat Klock on the wall

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.830
to Nancy and Jonathan.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.127
That clock is in the title
sequence for "Back to the Future"

00:01:14.127 --> 00:01:18.470
as well as in Katy Perry and
Taylor Swift music videos.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.540
There are also some visual parallels

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.568
between Joyce visiting
Mr. Clarke in his garage

00:01:23.568 --> 00:01:26.830
with Marty visiting Doc.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:28.780
There's been a poster for "The Thing"

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.620
on the Wheelers' basement
wall since season one,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.740
but the movie gets a
name check this season

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.880
by Mike and Lucas in episode three.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.540
In episode one, the gang
sneak into a screening

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.940
of George Romero's "Day of the Dead."

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.457
It's billed as a sneak preview,

00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:47.956
as the movie premiered on June 30, 1985,

00:01:47.956 --> 00:01:50.939
but had a widespread release on July 19,

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:53.764
so the Hawkins Middle AV
club really would have been

00:01:53.764 --> 00:01:56.520
some of the first people to watch it.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.353
We also see a poster for "The Evil Dead"

00:01:59.353 --> 00:02:01.828
on Jonathan's bedroom
wall in this episode,

00:02:01.828 --> 00:02:04.980
originating from 1981.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.088
Fans will be keeping a close eye out

00:02:07.088 --> 00:02:09.000
for the "It" Easter egg.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.120
In season two, Bob Newby's childhood story

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.253
seemed to recall the events
of Stephen King's "It."

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.670
Newby: Hey, kiddo, would
you like a balloon?

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.890
[Narrator] In season three, episode seven,

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.220
Alexei is shot in the
fairground by Grigori.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.630
At the same time, a red balloon is popped

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.840
by someone winning a prize.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.050
The book "It" didn't come out until 1986.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:35.870
Could the red balloon
pop be a nod to "It"?

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:39.941
The series makes heavy
reference to "The Goonies."

00:02:39.941 --> 00:02:41.896
Dustin also near repeats

00:02:41.896 --> 00:02:44.010
one of the most famous
lines in the film...

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.410
Sheriff: Holy Mary, mother of God.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.438
[Narrator] ...when arriving
at the bottom of the elevator

00:02:48.438 --> 00:02:50.310
in episode five.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.180
Interestingly, "The Goonies"
and "Stranger Things"

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.260
both starred Sean Astin,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.837
who played Bob Newby in season two

00:02:57.837 --> 00:03:01.183
and appears in a brief
season three flashback.

00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:02.940
The Soviet threat in season three

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.770
brings up some interesting parallels

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.453
with '80s Cold War movies.

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:12.453
The opening scenes resemble
that of 1983's "WarGames."

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:16.575
In episode five, Dustin
recalls the movie "Red Dawn,"

00:03:16.575 --> 00:03:19.570
in which a group of teenagers
attempt to defend their town

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.720
from a Russian invasion.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.010
In episode three, Dustin mentions to Steve

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.500
that they're likely to
spot an undercover Soviet,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.590
because he's carrying a duffel bag.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:30.932
And he's sort of got a point.

00:03:30.932 --> 00:03:33.315
Name any big action
movie from the mid-'80s,

00:03:33.315 --> 00:03:35.080
and this trope comes up.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.270
"Rambo: First Blood II," Soviets.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.350
"A View to a Kill," the Soviets.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:45.800
"Rocky IV," Dolph Lundgren as a Soviet.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.720
The plotline of Soviets
taking over a shopping mall

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.140
has parallels to Chuck Norris'
1985 film "Invasion USA,"

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.450
in which the action
star takes on a mixture

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:58.293
of Latin American and Soviet agents.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.670
In episode five, when
one of the Soviets shoots

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:04.100
at Joyce and Hopper, he follows the car

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.330
out into the garden in a robotic way.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.430
The scene looks very similar to the end

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:12.700
of the cop-shop shootout
in "The Terminator."

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.030
The mayor in episode
four even makes a joke

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.473
about one of the bad guys
being Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.990
In episode five, it's the most
obvious "Die Hard" reference.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.300
Hopper holds a gun to Grigori's head.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.790
Grigori says that Hopper
won't dare shoot him,

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:29.590
because policemen have rules...

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.976
John McClane: Yeah? Why not?

00:04:31.976 --> 00:04:33.684
Tony: Because you're a policeman.

00:04:33.684 --> 00:04:35.820
There are rules for policemen.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.662
[Narrator] ...an exact
quote a henchman says

00:04:37.662 --> 00:04:39.595
to John McClane, with the same spray

00:04:39.595 --> 00:04:41.911
of machine-gun fire after.

00:04:41.911 --> 00:04:43.610
Something more subtle, perhaps,

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:48.050
is Dustin getting stuck in
the air vent in episode four.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:50.880
Mid-'80s-horror fans will
love the funhouse sequence

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:52.521
in episode seven.

00:04:52.521 --> 00:04:54.112
When Hopper enters, you can hear

00:04:54.112 --> 00:04:56.353
a taunting, spooky voice say,

00:04:56.353 --> 00:05:00.300
"Do you dare face the
challenge of the funhouse?"

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.670
It sounds eerily like
the trailer voiceover

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:07.400
from 1981's "Funhouse," a
horror film set at the fair.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.360
The hall-of-mirrors moment plays out

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:13.410
like the 1983 fantasy "Something
Wicked This Way Comes,"

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:15.930
also set at the fairground.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.590
The Mind Flayer seeps and
oozes into host bodies,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.600
much like how The Blob moves
around in the 1988 movie.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.418
The way it hugs onto its
victim's face is similar,

00:05:26.418 --> 00:05:29.470
as well as its veiny, sticky tendrils.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.310
And that creepy hospital
scene in episode five?

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:36.580
It has a bit of a "Halloween
II" feeling about it.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.630
Episode six features a
rather gruesome scene,

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.021
where Steve and Robin are being

00:05:41.021 --> 00:05:42.971
interrogated by the Russians.

00:05:42.971 --> 00:05:44.800
Torture equipment is brought in,

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.940
along with an administrator
in a white overall.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.470
Personally, this reminded
me of the torture scene

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.920
in the 1985 Terry Gilliam film, "Brazil."

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.930
In episode eight, Billy's
car lights flash on

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:00.100
as he prepares to smash
into Jonathan and Nancy.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.150
These shots look very similar
to the 1983 horror film

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:07.340
"Christine," about a man
possessed by his car.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:09.930
Also, when he enters
the mall, Billy appears

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.660
to be dressed like Kurt Russell

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:15.840
in 1986's "Big Trouble in Little China."

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:18.380
In this same episode, Steve and Robin

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.730
are in the video-rental
store, where we can see

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.910
lots of posters and front
covers of VHS tapes.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:27.320
There's "Mad Max," "Animal
House," "Car Wash,"

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:31.640
as well as a poster in the
window for 1984's "Scarface."

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.142
Steve knocks over a cardboard cutout

00:06:34.142 --> 00:06:38.600
to 1982's "Fast Times at
Ridgemont High," which in itself

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:42.517
is a very Ridgemont High
slapstick thing to do.

00:06:44.552 --> 00:06:45.600
And we couldn't talk about movies

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.140
without mentioning "The
NeverEnding Story."

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.240
In episode eight, Dustin
calls his girlfriend, Susie,

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:53.465
to ask what Planck's constant is

00:06:53.465 --> 00:06:55.650
in order to unlock a door.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.530
She forces him to serenade her with

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.540
the theme tune to "The NeverEnding Story."

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.810
It was released in April 1985,

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:05.420
so it would have been
a current cinema movie.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:08.428
Also note how on the wall
of Susie's bedroom she has

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:12.420
a poster for "The Wizard of
Oz," another escapist fantasy.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.810
She's reading "A Wizard of
Earthsea" by Ursula Le Guin.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:20.750
Two popular '80s TV shows
feature in this season.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:24.260
In episode one, Hopper is
watching "Magnum P.I.,"

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.210
a show about a charismatic
private investigator.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.740
There's also a short snippet of "Cheers"

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.570
when Joyce is eating a TV dinner alone.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.427
She thinks about Bob commenting on

00:07:35.427 --> 00:07:38.267
the will-they-won't-they
storyline between the characters

00:07:38.267 --> 00:07:40.050
of Sam (Ted Danson) and
Diane (Shelley Long).

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:42.860
Could this be a parallel
to Joyce and Hopper's

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.660
will-they-won't-they sexual tension?

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.560
Woman: Ladies and
gentlemen, that concludes

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:49.990
the film portion of our presentation.

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.400
Now, I'm sure you all
have questions, so...

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.258
[Narrator] The shopping
mall was a central part

00:07:54.258 --> 00:07:56.270
of American life in the '80s.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:07:57.853
Jess Royal explained in

00:07:57.853 --> 00:08:00.445
"Stranger Things: Worlds
Turned Upside Down"

00:08:00.445 --> 00:08:02.419
that the production wanted to stay true

00:08:02.419 --> 00:08:04.210
to all the products on sale

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:07.070
so actually built a mall as the set,

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.853
genuinely filled with products.

00:08:09.853 --> 00:08:12.720
Classic 1980s US brands are in situ.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.600
There's The Gap, JCPenney,
Waldenbooks, Sam Goody,

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.620
and we're told that Taco Bell
and Esprit are coming soon.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.912
There's the book "Breaking with Moscow"

00:08:23.912 --> 00:08:25.370
behind Steve and Dustin

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:27.911
in this season three, episode three scene.

00:08:27.911 --> 00:08:30.520
This is a genuine 1985 release

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.390
by a former Soviet foreign minister.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.322
It foreshadows Alexei's storyline.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:42.300
In episode one's first
scene, after the credits,

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.600
the camera pans over to cassettes.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.075
One is by Bryan Adams,

00:08:46.075 --> 00:08:48.400
and the other one is by Corey Hart,

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.683
very much the 1983-84 heartthrob.

00:08:51.683 --> 00:08:53.682
It's also in this episode that we see

00:08:53.682 --> 00:08:56.430
music posters on Jonathan's bedroom wall.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.513
There's one for R.E.M.,

00:08:58.513 --> 00:09:01.646
specifically their 1983 release, "Murmur."

00:09:01.646 --> 00:09:04.037
Even though the band was formed in 1980,

00:09:04.037 --> 00:09:07.660
they didn't achieve vast
popularity until 1987,

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:12.100
which means Jonathan caught
them in their cult period.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:14.830
Eighties-era music fills the season.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:17.580
We catch parts of
Madonna's "Material Girl,"

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.410
REO Speedwagon's "Can't
Fight This Feeling,"

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.843
The Cars' "Moving in Stereo,"

00:09:22.843 --> 00:09:24.793
Foreigner's "Cold as Ice,"

00:09:24.793 --> 00:09:28.225
and Bonnie Tyler's "Total
Eclipse of the Heart."

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:30.969
Steve: Ahoy!

00:09:30.969 --> 00:09:31.860
Robin: Ahoy!

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:32.920
Man: Ahoy!

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.300
[Narrator] The season has
noticeable product placement

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.470
of Coca-Cola, specifically New Coke,

00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:42.480
which was a limited-edition run in 1985.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:44.560
Netflix executives even visited

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:47.210
the Atlanta Coca-Cola archives to study

00:09:47.210 --> 00:09:49.770
the packaging and advertising.

00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:50.990
Look closer at some of the books

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:53.580
and magazine covers throughout the season.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:56.650
In episode one, Mrs.
Wheeler is sat poolside

00:09:56.650 --> 00:10:00.160
reading Johanna Lindsey's
"Tender Is the Storm,"

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:03.690
a sensationalist romance novel from 1985.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.210
She also reads a book by the same author

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:09.720
in season two, episode nine.

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:13.240
In episode three, Max shows
Eleven a copy of Superteen

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.020
featuring a double-page
spread of Ralph Macchio,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:19.850
the Karate Kid, which came out in 1984.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:20.900
Daniel: Telling me.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:22.757
[Narrator] In Max's
bedroom, we see a poster

00:10:22.757 --> 00:10:26.920
for the 1960s cult surf
film "The Endless Summer."

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:28.311
The poster reads,

00:10:28.311 --> 00:10:30.902
"Follow the surf around
the world from Malibu,"

00:10:30.902 --> 00:10:32.418
which potentially foreshadows

00:10:32.418 --> 00:10:36.206
later surfing scenes in
California with Billy.

00:10:36.206 --> 00:10:39.225
The editions of Penthouse
magazine that Alex and Eleven

00:10:39.225 --> 00:10:41.733
discover in Billy's
drawer in episode three

00:10:41.733 --> 00:10:44.590
are genuine historical editions.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:48.630
In episode four, Max is also
reading mid-'80s editions

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:52.280
of "Wonder Woman" and "Green Lantern."

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:55.850
In episode seven, the group loots a store.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:58.116
Now, it's the same store
that Eleven stole from

00:10:58.116 --> 00:10:59.782
in season one.

00:10:59.782 --> 00:11:01.916
This time, she sits in front of a fridge

00:11:01.916 --> 00:11:03.270
full of Eggo waffles,

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.500
preparing to enter the Upside Down.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:07.715
In previous seasons, we have seen her go

00:11:07.715 --> 00:11:09.666
straight to grab them.

00:11:09.666 --> 00:11:11.916
But in season three, the
situation at the mall

00:11:11.916 --> 00:11:15.474
is so desperate that she
barely even looks at them.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.480
In Mike's basement, we
see retro board games

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:26.820
like Upwords, which was invented in 1981.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:29.963
I don't know about you, but
Dustin's toys coming to life

00:11:29.963 --> 00:11:31.170
reminds me of the front cover

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.660
of Stephen King's 1985
collection, "Skeleton Crew."

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:38.410
The cover features a
simple clapping monkey.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:40.150
Will appears in his wizard costume

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.900
for the first time during
Dungeons and Dragons,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:46.475
and it's a dead-on match with
the drawings of Will the Wise

00:11:46.475 --> 00:11:50.220
that we saw on the wall in
season two, episode four.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:53.490
The funfair scene gives
us two '80s references.

00:11:53.490 --> 00:11:55.790
The first is the ride the Gravitron,

00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:58.510
a popular ride introduced in 1983,

00:11:58.510 --> 00:12:01.730
and a stuffed toy Alexei
wins of Woody Woodpecker,

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:04.733
who was a kids-TV staple in the mid-'80s.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.715
Think you spotted anything not
on this list? Comment below.

